course: dictionary
lesson: 2926
title: Cold air and draughty rooms
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A draught is a current of cold air moving through a room. Draughty describes a room or building where that cold air blows in, usually through gaps round windows and doors. The -y at the end turns the noun into a describing word.
explain: Use the context to check. «Plain straw was spread on the stone floor of a draughty cell.» Stone floors are hard and cold, and the straw is only a thin layer, so the cell must have been cold and open to the air. Then the sheets and blankets felt «very friendly and comforting things», which tells you how cold it was.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it in. "The stone floor of a cold cell where air blows in through the gaps" still makes sense, so draughty is the right meaning.
fact: Draughty means having cold air blowing through a room or building from gaps round windows and doors.
fact: In American English the word is spelled drafty, but it means the same thing.
fact: A draught is a cold current of air; draught excluders are strips placed at the bottom of doors to stop it.
fact: Draughty is a common word for old houses, castles, halls and classrooms in winter.
fact: In science lessons on heat, a draughty room loses warm air through its gaps, so insulation helps keep the warmth in.
exercise: «The old castle was so draughty that the candles kept blowing out.» What does draughty mean? | Painted bright yellow and very clean | Full of people talking loudly | *Having cold air blowing through gaps | Very dark and quiet
exercise: Sam says draughty means warm and cosy. Is he right? | *No, a draughty room has cold air coming in through gaps | No, it means very quiet | Yes, a draughty room is always warm | Yes, because draughts are warm air
exercise: Find and copy the word that hints the floor was hard and cold: «plain straw... spread on the stone floor of a draughty cell» | plain | straw | spread | *stone
test: Why did the sheets and blankets feel «very friendly and comforting things»? | They were made of paper | *They were warm things in a cold, draughty cell | They were hidden under the bed | They were brand-new and bright
test: Which place is most likely to be draughty in winter? | A cupboard with the door shut tight | A warm, carpeted library with the heating on | A sealed car on a sunny road | *An old stone hall with gaps round the doors
exam: Which sentence uses draughty correctly? | Her sealed bedroom was draughty and perfectly warm | The hot bath was very draughty | *The old barn was so draughty that the children wore their coats indoors | The sunny beach felt draughty and calm
